# Rust Standards

Contents: Errors | Async | Boundaries and secrets | Exit codes | CLI contract | Structure | Dependencies | Tests

Touched code moves toward these rules. Do not add new violations; do not widen a change into a broad refactor.

## Errors

- Treat filesystem, object-store, catalog, subprocess, crypto, and config operations as fallible.
- No new `.unwrap()` or `.expect()` on runtime I/O paths. They are fine in `#[cfg(test)]` and in `LazyLock::new` with a clear message.
- `anyhow::Result` in `main.rs`, `cli/`, and top-level runners (`ingest::run`, `scan::run`, `profile::run`, `query::run`). Typed errors in `domain/`, `storage/`, `lakehouse/schema.rs`.
- At command boundaries add `.context` naming the operation and resource (path, bucket, table, endpoint).

## Async

- No blocking work inside `async fn`: no `std::fs`, sync `WalkDir`, or `std::process::Command` without `spawn_blocking` or tokio process APIs.
- CPU-heavy work (hashing, compression) runs in `spawn_blocking`.
- Object-store and catalog calls need explicit timeouts.
- External tools (`exiftool`, `ffprobe`, `pdfinfo`) need bounded execution.
- Never hold a mutex or RwLock guard across `.await`.

## Boundaries and secrets

- Object-store access and HTTP boundaries: `.cursor/rules/project-architecture.mdc`.
- Keep domain logic apart from CLI, I/O, and subprocess code. Prefer typed domain values over raw `String`, `bool`, `PathBuf` across layers.
- No secret fields in `Debug` or `Display`; use redacted types. No credentials in logs or CLI output.

## Exit codes

- Mutating commands with per-item failures exit non-zero unless a partial-success policy is documented and tested.
- Catalog/store inconsistency fails the command.
- Non-dry-run ingest and query preflight the store and catalog, or document the gap.

## CLI contract

- Every public flag has runtime behavior, tests, and docs. No inert flags.
- Mutating commands support `--dry-run` or say "not implemented".
- Placeholder commands exit non-zero.
- Help text, behavior, tests, and docs agree.

## Structure

- Do not grow `lakehouse/mod.rs`; put new HTTP, signing, or init code in focused modules.
- Prefer enums over boolean parameters that select behavior.
- Keep visibility minimal; prefer `pub(crate)` over `pub`.
- Comments explain invariants and tradeoffs, not the code. A TODO needs a reason and a tracking reference.
- No broad `#[allow(dead_code)]`; a narrow one needs an inline reason.
- Use `tracing` for I/O diagnostics in library modules; leave existing `println!` alone unless migrating it is the task.

## Dependencies

- Check `Cargo.toml` before adding a `use`. Prefer `std`, then existing crates; ask before heavy additions.
- Remove unused crates when touched, or say why they stay.

## Tests

- Unit-test pure logic in `domain/`, `config/`, `lakehouse/schema.rs`.
- Stack tests are `#[ignore]` tests in `tests/cli_tests.rs`, run against Compose by `.github/workflows/stack-tests.yml`. Name and document them; do not claim end-to-end coverage from unit tests. No testcontainers without approval.
- Use `tempfile`, never hardcoded machine paths.
- `#[tokio::test]` for async, `#[test]` for sync.
- Assert both the positive and the negative case for changed user-facing behavior.
